\section{Intended backgrounds}{

\code{coral_night} is intended for a black or dark background; \code{coral_day}, \code{axmanesque_white} and \code{depthmapx_classic_white} for a white
or light one; the depthmapX modes carry no stated background. The \code{background} column of \code{colour_palettes()} records this
intention. \strong{No function in rPSTool changes a plot's background}: choosing a palette never alters the theme.

The Coral palettes give an overall dark-to-light (Night) or light-to-dark (Day) progression of values with visibly distinct
colour transitions; the anchors are ordered in lightness, with small lightness dips along the continuous curves. The white
adaptations reduce the visual weight of dark blues and strong reds on white while retaining recognisable depthmapX hue
sequences; they still contain bright cyan and green and uneven lightness. No palette has been tested with observers, and
colour-blind safety, perceptual uniformity and reading accuracy have not been assessed. The depthmapX source comments
describe its Blue-Red and Purple-Orange arrays as red-green colour-blind safe; rPSTool has not verified this.

The historical colour palettes follow the colour conventions of Axman and Depthmap (Dalton, 1988; Turner, 2001). The Axmanesque
palette takes its name from Axman. rPSTool implements the palettes in R using hexadecimal colour values and independently written
colour mapping functions. The palettes are documented in the repository
(\url{https://github.com/kimonkrenz/rPSTool/blob/main/docs/palettes.md}). The Blue-Red and
Purple-Orange colour values coincide, in reversed order, with the 7-class ColorBrewer RdYlBu and PuOr schemes (Brewer et al.);
the colours are attributed to ColorBrewer (Cynthia Brewer).
}
